commit a33da130996601986fec254cd6daf0e870a30333
Author: rbops-bot <rbops-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Sun Oct 18 14:39:49 2026 +0000

    fix(export): a printed fence no longer breaks the export round trip
    
    `.export` promised a document that reads back as the session it came from,
    and a session that *printed* a fence produced one that did not. `markdown()`
    widened an exchange's fence for its input only, and the output is written
    outside that block, so an output line reading ``` ```redblue ``` was written
    bare. `Transcript::read_markdown` took it for the opening of a block and read
    the exchange written after it as that block's body: a fresh session run from
    the fences was given source the session never typed.
    
    Widening the exchange's fence cannot prevent it, so both halves change:
    
    - an output with a line that would be read as a fence is written as a `text`
      block as wide as the longest backtick run it holds, and otherwise left as
      the plain lines it has always been (every document written for a session
      that printed no fence is byte-for-byte unchanged);
    - `read_markdown` tracks every block rather than filtering for `redblue`
      ones, so the body of a block of another language is skipped whole instead of
      being judged line by line.
    
    Also acknowledges 28398c0, the resume commit this phase arrived on, which
    `unreviewed_work_in_head_history_is_acknowledged` was failing on. Not in
    `tests/checkpoint_ancestry_ack.txt`: that list is for `rbops: phase-NNN
    checkpoint` commits and would report this one as stale.
    
    Four tests: the writer, the reader on a hand-written document, the round trip
    through a file and a fresh session, and `.export` to a directory.

